      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": ">  I think this is limited - an attacker can still fingerprint based on observed behaviour in many cases\n\nI agree, but I wanted to see how easy it is, so I put my LLM to work:\nHere is a list of the most important ones per version that are not invasive and quick to check (there are more, plust I didn't verify everything yet):\n\n0.19 -> 0.20: getdata for unknown MSG_WTX hash: stalls -> silently dropped (weak, some older versions also drop)\n0.20 -> 0.21: protocol version 70015 -> 70016 (wtxid relay)\n0.21 -> 22: sendcmpct sent before verack: ignored -> honoured\n22 -> 23: unsolicited addr before we send any address message: sent -> not sent\n23 -> 24: sendcmpct messages in handshake: two -> one\n24 -> 25: 65-byte tx accepted as orphan: no -> yes\n25 -> 26: getheaders locator version when we advertise 70015: 70015 -> 70016\n26 -> 27: none found\n27 -> 28: nine unconnecting headers messages: eight answered with getheaders -> all nine answered\n28 -> 29: orphan with dust output: rejected -> accepted\n29 -> 30: send orphan with two OP_RETURN outputs: rejected -> accepted\n30 -> 31: self-announcement after getaddr: inside the response -> separate one-entry addr message\n31 -> 32: protocol version 70016 -> 70017 (feature message)\n\nI then let my LLM build a tool based on these signals and others, and connected to all reachable  nodes and compared the detected with the advertised version - still analyzing the data, but it seems that most Core nodes don't appear to lie about their major version, and if they do they run custom software instead of a different version. \n\nSo I think removing the user agent from major versions would not help all that much against an attacker - they can easily build a similar tool if they want to, I on purpose didn't put any of my own knowledge of the p2p code into any of this. Removing minor versions on the other hand is something that I think could make sense.",
